diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 5e414f3..6d3e605 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@ src/main/java/com/example/
   Application.java                    Spring Boot entry point
   customers/
     domain/Customer.java              the record shown in the grid
+    domain/CustomerFilter.java        the name and status criteria a customer must meet
     domain/CustomerRepository.java    paged, read-only access to customers.csv
     domain/CustomerSort.java          a sort instruction the repository understands
     service/CustomerService.java      what the UI talks to
@@ -17,6 +18,21 @@ src/main/java/com/example/
 The 500 customers live in `src/main/resources/customers.csv` and are loaded once
 at startup.

+## Filtering
+
+The view at `/` has two filters, applied by the backend so the grid stays lazily
+loaded:
+
+- **Name:** the text is split on whitespace, and every term must appear in the
+  first or last name. Case and accents are ignored, so `makinen` matches `Mäkinen`.
+- **Status:** with nothing selected every status is shown; otherwise only the
+  selected ones are.
+
+The filter is also in the URL as the query parameters `name` and `status`, for
+example `/?name=vir&status=ACTIVE,CLOSED`. Opening a URL with those parameters
+applies the filter. Changing the filter replaces the current history entry
+instead of adding one per keystroke.
+
 `CustomerRepository` deliberately behaves like a real paged backend: it refuses
